Body:
Another possible fix that is lighter than force-remount: deferring the `onSelect` callback via `queueMicrotask` so Kobalte fully completes its Portal cleanup before any reactive state update runs.

In `packages/ui/src/v2/components/select-v2.tsx` and `packages/ui/src/components/select.tsx`:

```ts
// Before
onChange={(next) => {
  const v = next == null ? null : Array.isArray(next) ? ((next[0] as T) ?? null) : (next as T)
  local.onSelect?.(v)
  stop()
}}

// After
onChange={(next) => {
  const v = next == null ? null : Array.isArray(next) ? ((next[0] as T) ?? null) : (next as T)
  queueMicrotask(() => local.onSelect?.(v))
  stop()
}}
```

Rationale: In Kobalte's `onSelectionChange`, `onChange` fires first (triggering user callback + SolidJS store update), then `close()` runs (closing dropdown, portal cleanup). Both updates land in the same SolidJS batch. When the batch flushes, DOM modifications from the store's effect (e.g. theme CSS updating `<style>` element `textContent`) collide with Kobalte's portal cleanup/close animation, corrupting the Portal's internal state.

By deferring the user callback to the next microtask, Kobalte finishes `close()` → portal cleanup → batch flush completely first, THEN the reactive update runs separately. This avoids the collision without needing to unmount/remount the component.

Would be interested to hear if this resolves the issue for anyone who can test it.
